{"module": "blackout", "source_id": "AFTD2022-EXP-fc1ed73935da2203", "guideline_quote": "Blackouts may occur due to a range of mechanisms including: • vasovagal syncope or ‘faint’, which accounts for more than 50 per cent of blackouts and may be due to factors such as hot weather, emotion or venepuncture but may also be due to more serious causes that may recur • syncope due to other cardiovascular causes such as structural heart disease, arrhythmias or vascular disease • epileptic seizure, which accounts for less than 10 per cent of blackouts • other causes including metabolic causes (e.g. hypoglycaemia), drug intoxication or a sleep disorder.", "fact_fields": ["blackout.diagnosis"], "licence_pathway": "conditional", "finding": "established_restriction", "conclusion": "The patient's blackout is due to hypoglycaemia, which is a medical cause not covered in the standards."}, {
"module": "blackout", "source_id": "AFTD2022-EXP-2332d9548f2abd01", "guideline_quote": "If the cause of the blackout is determined to be a medical condition not covered in the standards, then first principles regarding fitness to drive should be applied (refer to Part A section 2. Assessing fitness to drive – general guidance).", "fact_fields": ["blackout.diagnosis"], "licence_pathway": "conditional", "finding": "established_restriction", "conclusion": "The patient's blackout is due to hypoglycaemia, which is a medical condition not covered in the standards."}, {
"module": "blackout", "source_id": "AFTD2022-EXP-bbf242d555576d02", "guideline_quote": "A person should not drive for 5 years following a single blackout of undetermined nature. A person should not drive for 10 years following two or more blackouts of undetermined nature separated by a 24-hour period.", "fact_fields": ["blackout.occurred"], "licence_pathway": "unconditional", "finding": "not_established", "conclusion": "The patient's blackout is diagnosed, so the unconditional standard does not apply."}, {
"module": "blackout", "source_id": "AFTD2022-EXP-05643c50d6cf5973", "guideline_quote": "Some drivers may attribute a crash or driving mishap to a ‘blackout’ to excuse an event that occurred for some other reason such as inattention or distraction (e.g. a mobile phone conversation).", "fact_fields": ["blackout.occurred"], "licence_pathway": "unconditional", "finding": "not_established", "conclusion": "The patient's blackout is diagnosed, so the unconditional standard does not apply."}, {
"module": "blackout", "source_id": "AFTD2022-BLK-COM-006", "guideline_quote": "themselves with the information in this chapter and the tabulated standards before assessing a person’s fitness to drive. Where a firm diagnosis has been made, the standard appropriate to the condition should be referred to in this publication (refer to Figure 6. Management of blackouts and driving). For blackouts due to medical causes not covered in the standards, refer to first principles (refer to Part A section 2. Assessing fitness to drive – general guidance). For blackouts where, after investigation, it is not possible to diagnose one of the conditions covered elsewhere in this publication, refer to the table for blackouts of uncertain nature over the page.", "fact_fields": ["blackout.diagnosis"], "licence_pathway": "conditional", "finding": "established_restriction", "conclusion": "The patient's blackout is diagnosed, and the guidelines suggest applying the standard appropriate to the condition."}]}

